// math-demo — a single comprehensive tour of texish mathematics.
//
// Everything here is the engine's own math mode — fractions, roots, scripts, big
// operators, matrices, the math-array environments, the stacking and framing boxes,
// the math alphabets, the operator names, the modular forms and the implication
// arrows. None of it needs a package.
//
// \use{document} is only for the title block, numbered sections and prose styling.

\use{document}

\title {A Tour of Mathematics in texish}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

This document exercises the whole of texish math mode in one place, all of it
built into the engine and none of it needing a package. Every formula below is
live — what you see is what the engine set.

\section {Inline and display math}

Math is written between dollar signs. A single pair, $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, sets the
formula \italic{inline}, in the run of the text and on the current line. A double
pair sets it as a centred \italic{display}, broken out of the paragraph and given
room to breathe:

$$e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0.$$

The same notation reads both ways: the inline limit $\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n = L$
keeps its subscript tucked beside the operator to save vertical space, while the
display form lets it drop underneath.

\section {Style}

A formula is set in one of four styles — display, text, script and scriptscript —
and the style decides both the type size and how scripts are placed. That is why
the same sum reads differently in the two contexts above. The four style
declarations ask for one directly, for the rest of the enclosing sub-formula, so a
formula in running text can be given the room of a display:

The sum $\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i$ stacks its bounds even here, in the
middle of a paragraph, because the formula opens with a \mono{\\displaystyle}.
Without it the same sum sets as $\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i$, bounds tucked beside it.

Braces bound the switch, since a group in math is a sub-formula of its own. The
reverse works too — one part of a display can be held back to text size while
everything around it stays large:

$$\sum_{i=1}^{n} {\textstyle \frac{1}{2}} x_i
  \qquad
  {\scriptstyle \text{small}} \; {\scriptscriptstyle \text{smaller}}.$$

\section {Fractions and binomials}

The plain \mono{\\frac} chooses its size from context — full size in a display,
small in a line of text. The display fractions \mono{\\dfrac} and \mono{\\tfrac}
fix that choice regardless of where they sit, and \mono{\\binom} stacks a
binomial coefficient inside invisible delimiters:

$$\frac{a}{b} + \frac{c}{d} = \frac{ad + bc}{bd}
  \qquad
  \binom{n}{k} = \dfrac{n!}{k!\,(n - k)!}.$$

Because \mono{\\dfrac} stays full size everywhere, it nests cleanly into a
continued fraction, while \mono{\\tfrac} keeps a one-line fraction compact:

$$\dfrac{1}{1 + \dfrac{1}{1 + \dfrac{1}{1 + \dfrac{1}{x}}}}
  \qquad
  \tfrac{1}{2} + \tfrac{1}{3} = \tfrac{5}{6}.$$

Those are the combinations common enough to have names. Underneath them is one
general form: \mono{\\frac} takes the bar thickness, a pair of fences and the
style as parameters, which between them cover every fraction-like stack — a bare
stack at \mono{rule:0}, a binomial by adding fences, a heavier bar for emphasis:

$$\frac rule:0 {n}{k}
  \qquad
  \frac left:( right:) rule:0 {n}{k}
  \qquad
  \frac rule:1.2pt {a}{b}
  \qquad
  \frac style:display {1}{2}.$$

\section {Roots}

A square root sets a surd over its argument, sized to fit; an optional index
turns it into an $n$th root:

$$\sqrt{2} \qquad \sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \qquad \sqrt[3]{x} \qquad \sqrt[n]{\frac{a}{b}}.$$

Roots and fractions compose to any depth, the radical stretching to clear what it
covers:

$$\varphi = \frac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}
  \qquad
  \sqrt{1 + \sqrt{1 + \sqrt{1 + x}}}.$$

\section {Scripts and big operators}

Subscripts and superscripts attach with the underscore and caret, and group
with braces when they run to more than a token:

$$x_i^2 \qquad a_{n+1} \qquad e^{x^2} \qquad 2^{2^{2^{2}}}.$$

The big operators — sums, products and integrals — carry their bounds as scripts.
In a display they sit above and below the operator; the integral keeps its bounds
to the side:

$$\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}
  \qquad
  \prod_{k=1}^{n} k = n!
  \qquad
  \int_0^1 x^2 \, dx = \frac{1}{3}.$$

They nest and stack as expected:

$$\sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{m} a_{ij}
  \qquad
  \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2} \, dx = \sqrt{\pi}.$$

\section {Greek, operators and relations}

Greek letters are named directly, lower and upper case:

$$\alpha \; \beta \; \gamma \; \delta \; \theta \; \lambda \; \mu \; \pi \; \sigma \; \omega
  \qquad
  \Gamma \; \Delta \; \Theta \; \Lambda \; \Pi \; \Sigma \; \Omega.$$

The named functions are set upright, like \mono{\\sin}, \mono{\\log} and
\mono{\\lim}, and take operator spacing. The inverse trigonometric and
probability names sit in the same set:

$$\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1
  \qquad
  \arctan x = \int_0^x \frac{dt}{1 + t^2}
  \qquad
  \Pr(A \cup B) \le \Pr(A) + \Pr(B).$$

When a function has no built-in name, \mono{\\operatorname} makes one — the run
is kept roman and given the spacing of an operator, with its scripts set to the
side like the log-like \mono{\\sin} and \mono{\\arccos}, not stacked beneath:

$$\operatorname{argmax}_{x} f(x)
  \qquad
  \arcsin x + \arccos x = \frac{\pi}{2}.$$

The modular forms are built in too. \mono{\\bmod} is the binary "mod"
of $a \bmod b$, set with a medium space on each side; \mono{\\pmod} closes a
congruence with a parenthesised modulus after a quad of space:

$$15 \bmod 4 = 3
  \qquad
  17 \equiv 2 \pmod{5}
  \qquad
  a \equiv b \mod n.$$

Relations and set notation carry their own spacing too:

$$a < b \le c \qquad x \in \mathbb{R} \qquad A \subseteq B \cup C
  \qquad
  f : X \to Y.$$

\section {Matrices}

The matrix environments share one layout and differ only in the fence they put
around it — none, parentheses, brackets, braces or bars:

$$\begin{matrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{matrix}
  \qquad
  \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{pmatrix}
  \qquad
  \begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{bmatrix}
  \qquad
  \begin{Bmatrix} x \\ y \end{Bmatrix}.$$

The bar fences give the determinant and the norm:

$$\det A = \begin{vmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{vmatrix} = ad - bc
  \qquad
  \begin{Vmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{Vmatrix}.$$

A \mono{smallmatrix} carries no fences of its own, so you bracket it yourself;
it sets compactly inside a line, as in
$(\begin{smallmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{smallmatrix})$.

\section {Cases and aligned blocks}

A piecewise definition uses \mono{cases}, whose rows are set flush left under a
single brace, the column before \mono{\\&} the value and the column after it the
condition:

$$|x| = \begin{cases} \phantom{-}x & x \ge 0, \\ -x & x < 0. \end{cases}$$

The \mono{aligned} environment lines a block of equations up on their relation
symbols — the \mono{\\&} marks the alignment column, just before the relation:

$$\begin{aligned}
  (a + b)^2 &= a^2 + 2ab + b^2 \\
            &= a^2 + b^2 + 2ab.
\end{aligned}$$

The \mono{gathered} environment centres a run of equations without aligning
them, and \mono{split} breaks one long equation across lines on a shared
alignment point:

$$\begin{gathered}
  a^2 + b^2 = c^2 \\
  E = mc^2
\end{gathered}
\qquad
\begin{split}
  f(x) &= (x+1)(x-1) \\
       &= x^2 - 1.
\end{split}$$

\section {Stacks, overlines and boxes}

\mono{\\overset} and \mono{\\underset} stack an annotation above or below a
base, and \mono{\\substack} stacks the lines of a many-line subscript:

$$A \overset{\text{def}}{=} B
  \qquad
  \underset{n \to \infty}{\lim} \frac{1}{n} = 0
  \qquad
  \sum_{\substack{0 < i < n \\ i \ne k}} a_i.$$

A rule may be drawn across a whole sub-formula with \mono{\\overline} or
\mono{\\underline}, and \mono{\\boxed} rings a result in a frame:

$$\overline{a + b} = \overline{a} + \overline{b}
  \qquad
  \underline{x + y}
  \qquad
  \boxed{\, E = mc^2 \,}.$$

\section {Delimiters and fences}

\mono{\\left} and \mono{\\right} grow a pair of delimiters to cover whatever sits
between them, which is the right rule whenever something does:

$$\left( \frac{a}{b} \right)
  \qquad
  \left[ \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i \right]
  \qquad
  \left\{ \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{x}} \right\}.$$

A fence that stands on its own has nothing to be sized from — the bar of a
set-builder, a divider between alternatives, an opening bracket whose partner is a
line away. \mono{\\fence} sets one at a size you choose: \mono{size:0} is the plain
glyph and each step climbs to the font's next larger form.

$$\{\, x \fence{|} x > 0 \,\}
  \qquad
  \fence size:1 {(} a \fence size:1 {)}
  \qquad
  \fence size:2 {(} b \fence size:2 {)}
  \qquad
  \fence size:3 {(} c \fence size:3 {)}.$$

The space around a lone fence follows from the delimiter itself — an opener keeps
none from what follows it, while a symmetric fence like the bar above keeps a
relation's space on both sides.

\section {Braces over and under}

A horizontal brace grows to span whatever it covers, and a script attached to it
rides centred over or under the brace rather than beside it — which is how the
brace gets its label:

$$\overbrace{a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n}^{n \text{ terms}}
  \qquad
  \underbrace{x \cdot x \cdots x}_{k \text{ factors}} = x^k.$$

\section {Boxes on the math axis}

The math axis is the invisible line a fraction bar sits on and a fence centres
about. \mono{\\vcenter} sets a box there instead of on the baseline, so material
built out of ordinary boxes reads level with the formula beside it:

$$T = \vcenter{\hbox{first line}\hbox{second line}} \ne \hbox{on the baseline}.$$

\section {Numbered displays}

\mono{\\eqno} flushes an equation number to the right margin of a display line and
\mono{\\leqno} to the left; the formula stays centred on the measure either way.
Which side a document numbers on is a house style, set once by the macro that
wraps the display:

$$x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \eqno(1)$$

$$e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0 \leqno(2)$$

\section {Math alphabets}

A formula draws its variables from the Mathematical Alphanumeric blocks of the
math font. Each alphabet command remaps the letters of its argument into one of
those blocks, so the same word can be set in any of the standard math typefaces:

$$\mathrm{Roman} \quad \mathbf{Bold} \quad \mathit{Italic}
  \quad \mathsf{Sans} \quad \mathtt{Typewriter}$$

The two decorative alphabets mathematicians reach for constantly are blackboard
bold, for the number systems, and calligraphic and fraktur, for structures and
ideals:

$$\mathbb{N} \subset \mathbb{Z} \subset \mathbb{Q} \subset \mathbb{R} \subset \mathbb{C}$$

$$\mathcal{F} : \mathfrak{A} \to \mathfrak{B}, \qquad \mathfrak{p} \subset \mathcal{O}_K$$

A worked sentence mixes several at once: let $\mathbb{F}$ be a field, $V$ a vector
space over $\mathbb{F}$, and $\mathcal{L}(V)$ the algebra of operators on it;
write $\mathbf{x}$ for a column vector and $\mathrm{tr}\, A$ for a trace.

\section {Text, connectives and ellipses}

\mono{\\text} drops a run of ordinary upright prose into a formula, the way a
qualifying phrase reads beside an equation:

$$x^2 = 4 \quad \text{if and only if} \quad x = \pm 2.$$

The implication arrows join whole statements, each a heavy arrow
with a thick space on either side, so a chain of reasoning reads naturally:

$$x^2 = 4 \implies x = 2 \;\text{or}\; x = -2 \iff |x| = 2.$$

An ellipsis carries a sequence to its limit. \mono{\\dots} chooses its height
from what follows, as in amsmath: centred dots before an operator or relation,
so they line up with the plus signs, and low dots before a comma, on the
baseline with them. (\mono{\\cdots} and \mono{\\ldots} force either form.)

$$1 + 2 + 3 + \dots + n = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}
  \qquad
  a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n.$$

\section {Forcing a symbol's class}

Every symbol in a formula belongs to a class — ordinary, operator, binary,
relation, opening, closing, punctuation or inner — and the class sets the spacing
around it. The eight class-forcing commands override that class when a symbol is
being used in an unusual role. For instance, a slash used as a binary operator
takes the medium binary spacing with \mono{\\mathbin}, while a colon forced to a
relation takes the wider relation spacing with \mono{\\mathrel}:

$$a \mathbin{/} b \qquad x \mathrel{:} y \qquad \mathopen| x \mathclose|.$$

The full set is \mono{\\mathord}, \mono{\\mathop}, \mono{\\mathbin},
\mono{\\mathrel}, \mono{\\mathopen}, \mono{\\mathclose}, \mono{\\mathpunct}
and \mono{\\mathinner}.

\vfill\eject
